## Major win: cache_coherency PASSES 4/4 — the 15+ session blocker is closed

**Root cause found and fixed (RULE 4, fully proven in one probe build):** `test_cross_write_read` failed because a CREATE satisfied from the inode cache (a reused inode number) keeps the prior incarnation's DLM fields — `i_dlm_mode=EX` with `i_dlm_unpublished=false` — while `xfs_inactive` had already released the prior incarnation's on-disk CAW slot. `mxfs_dlm_publish_inode` fast-bailed on the clear flag, so the creator ran on a **phantom EX with no slot**: readers' acquires of the reused number granted clean against an empty slot, never BASTed the writer, and adopted the new dinode at its not-yet-durable size=0 — empty md5 reads. Probes `P128-PUBLISH-BAIL`/`P128-INACT-EXREL` proved the chain (writer bail on ino 139 correlated exactly with three readers' size=0 adopts). Fix: `mxfs_dlm_rearm_unpublished()` called from `xfs_iget` on cache-hit `XFS_IGET_CREATE` (build `CA701441`). Full criterion: **PASS 4/4**.

## Ship gate progress: 9/19, then a new pre-existing blocker

Ran `verify_ship.sh` end-to-end: criteria 1–9 PASS. Criterion 10, `posix_semantics --nodes 1`, dies on its 600s watchdog: **`test_many_files` deterministically wedges a single node** — inodegc workers stuck forever in `xfs_ail_push_all_sync` behind one un-flushable AIL inode item (ino 6291584, li_buf attached, unpinned, not flushing — evidence captured via a new `P128-AILSTUCK` dump). This is pre-existing (my fixes are multi-node-gated; posix_semantics has no prior recorded run). Next step is instrumenting `xfs_inode_item_push` to see which branch refuses the flush.

